<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alessandro Sforza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1473">1473</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alessandro Sforza was a condottiero and the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In 1445 he bought Pesaro from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-malatesta">Galeazzo Malatesta</a>, whose granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costanza-da-varano">Costanza da Varano</a> he had married, and he ruled it as papal vicar until his death. He made Pesaro a cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> court, and his descendants held the lordship until 1512.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alessandro-sforza.jpg" alt="Alessandro Sforza" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rogier_van_der_weyden_(bottega),_trittico_sforza,_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alessandro Sforza was born in Cotignola on 21 October 1409, an illegitimate son of the condottiero Muzio Attendolo Sforza and Lucia Terzani de Martini of Torgiano. He was first named Gregorio, and the name was changed to Alessandro in honour of the antipope Alexander V. He was the youngest of the sixteen known children of his father, who married the noblewoman Antonia Salimbeni instead of Lucia and arranged for her to wed one of his captains, Mario Fogligno of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. Alessandro and his elder brother were therefore brought up in Ferrara, where their mother lived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He fought alongside his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> in his campaigns. In 1435 he won the battle at Fiordimonte in which Niccolò Fortebraccio was killed, and in 1442 he commanded the troops besieged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a> by Francesco Piccinino, condottiero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a>, and was forced to abandon the city to sack and massacre. At Pesaro he enlarged the Ducal Palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> manner. During the wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> he governed Parma and in February 1446 proclaimed himself its lord, a title the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> confirmed in 1454. In 1464 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> granted him the lordship of Gradara, which he defended against the Malatesta.</p><p>In 1465 the condottiero Jacopo Piccinino was arrested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and died in obscure circumstances, and suspicion fell on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>. Piccinino's pregnant widow Drusiana, Francesco's illegitimate daughter, took refuge with her uncle Alessandro at Teramo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He married Costanza Varano, daughter of Pietro Gentile I da Varano, on 8 December 1444; she died giving birth to their son Costanzo. He then married Sveva da Montefeltro, daughter of Guidantonio da Montefeltro, but in 1457, fearing a Malatesta plot to regain Pesaro, forced her to become a nun. Their daughter Battista married Federico III of Urbino, and his illegitimate daughter Ginevra, a patron of the arts, married two Bentivoglio lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. He died of apoplexy on 3 April 1473 and was succeeded at Pesaro by Costanzo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alessandro Sforza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1445 Galeazzo Malatesta sold Pesaro to <b>Alessandro Sforza</b>, brother of Francesco Sforza, who had married his granddaughter Costanza da Varano.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-pesaro">Lordship of Pesaro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cotignola, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pesaro, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.38,11.93&amp;year=1450" aria-label="Cotignola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4835 594 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alessandro Sforza"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 864h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 864h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.38,11.93&amp;year=1450">What was here in 1450? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
